          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar because the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a state called insulin resistance. The pancreas cannot keep up with the extra insulin output that this resistance demands, so blood glucose stays above the normal range. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThis differs from type 1 diabetes, where the insulin-producing cells are destroyed and insulin is absent rather than ineffective. The condition usually develops gradually over years. Many people have no symptoms when it is diagnosed. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages did not explain the detailed biology behind insulin resistance, such as why tissues become resistant. If you want to know more, a clinician or diabetes educator is a good person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
